  "era": "Designed 1936, built 1937 (architect Dov Karmi, engineer Zvi Barak); room book surveyed 2015",
  "source": {
   "title": "Max Liebling House conservation survey (Brenne Architekten for the Tel Aviv-Yafo Foundation, Getty 'Keeping It Modern'): Fig. 70 third-floor plan, PDF p. 44, 0-5 m scale bar; schedule of rooms 02.A-02.J, pp. 209-226; window and door type tables, sections 3.8-3.9; east elevation Fig. 79",
   "url": "https://media.getty.edu/Text/f08fb0ee-ac1d-571a-95c2-a32dc1c7c645.pdf",
   "license": "Published plan, used only as a reference for dimensions"
  },
  "assumptions": [
   "Walls read from Fig. 70 rendered at 300 dpi with the plan's own scale bar (88.6 px/m), about +-0.1 m; the box 13.36 x 11.39 m runs from the outer face of room A's west wall to the east facade and from the party wall with the rear flat's kitchen R to the outer face of room C's south wall",
   "Title gives no room count: the survey says 'three rooms' (pp. 22 and 42) and 'four-room apartments' (p. 41); the plan shows rooms A-D, with B opened to the hall by a glass partition (a former waiting room)",
   "Room uses are assumed (the survey names rooms by letter only): A living room, B study, C and D bedrooms; all loose furniture is assumed, the built-ins (drywall shelving in A and C, hall closet S.6, kitchen cabinet S.5 and counter, bathtub) are from the schedule of rooms; the fridge stands in the kitchen recess the room book says was 'possibly used for a refrigerator'; the server rack on room D's wall c is in the room book, its size (60 x 42 x 60 cm, wall-mounted at 1.6 m) and spot on the wall are assumed",
   "Hall H / room B is modelled as the survey describes it (rooms 02.B and 02.H): a solid dwarf wall, assumed 1.0 m high and 12 cm block, with aluminium-framed clear glazing above it up to the 35 cm beam and a glazed single-leaf door, open; at the 1 m coverage plane the signal crosses it through the glass",
   "One own loss only: the 0.45 m concrete pier at the H/B/I corner (concrete_20 wall) takes 20 dB at 5 GHz, the shared table's 'column' item (ITU-R P.526 estimate: a pier this narrow is passed round), instead of the 30.9 dB of a continuous 20 cm RC wall; every other wall, door and window uses the shared table",
   "Interior walls (coarse sand block, 6 or 12 cm, plastered: survey p. 41) use block_10, 12 dB at 5 GHz; the table has no 6 cm block, so the thin walls may lose less (ITU-R P.2040 brick gives about 4 dB for 10 cm, homes council minutes): the 'block_10' choice is the cautious end. Sensitivity (5 GHz, router at the entrance): with 4 dB instead of 12 dB per block_10 wall the indoor floor at -67 dBm or better goes from 61% to 87%",
   "Stairwell / room A wall is concrete_20: the room book calls wall 02.A-a 'interior wall, presumably concrete'; the 0.45 m pier at the H/B/I corner is the 'plastered concrete column' of rooms 02.B and 02.H",
   "Exterior walls (silicate brick / coarse sand block on an RC frame, 25-30 cm drawn) use brick_bauhaus; the 12 cm walls onto the utility balcony use block_10",
   "Window and balcony-door sizes from the survey's window type table (section 3.8, cm); interior door widths read from the plan (the door type table gives no sizes): A.02 158x209, C.04 82x209, G.10 70x209, A.01 and C.05 148x113, C.06 and D.07 179x67, E.08 110x99, F.09 40x100, G.12 43x122, D.27 93x26, V.26 209x125",
   "Sills: C.06 and D.07 at 1.40 m measured on the east elevation (Fig. 79); the other windows are assumed to share the 2.09 m door head (sill 0.96 for 113 cm windows); B.03 (new aluminium slider, size not given) is assumed 1.45 x 1.13 m like the living-room windows; D.27 borrowed light sill 2.20 m assumed",
   "Glass: single clear glass in wooden frames (survey) = 'glass' fill",
   "Trisim in daily use (daytime): living room A and study B rolled up, bedrooms C and D down with the slits open; the kitchen, WC and bath have slatted folding shutters, not modelled",
   "Doors in daily use: flat entrance (solid oak, D.1.2) closed, WC and bath closed, the others open",
   "Loggia parapet 1.0 m high (rendered parapet, height not given), open above; loggia dropped ceiling 2.75 m",
   "Kitchen-to-utility-balcony door G.10 closed; the laundry closet on the balcony and the radiators are not modelled",
   "Router (ISP default, position assumed): in hall H on the stairwell wall just beside the flat door, where a line from the stairwell would enter, 1.5 m high, 5 GHz, 20 dBm. The room book also shows an electrical distribution box in corridor I (02.I, wall b) and a server rack on room D's wall c. Computed alternatives (indoor floor at -67 dBm or better): entrance 61%, distribution box in corridor I 67% (bedrooms C and D better, living room about the same at -69 dBm median, loggia -77), rack in room D 50%",
   "Stairwell and the strip of the rear flat inside the box are marked outside, so the shares count only the flat (with its loggia and the utility balcony)",
   "Ceiling 3.10 m (survey p. 22); the stairwell's curved north-west corner is drawn square"
  ],
  "israeli_features": [
   "Tel Aviv White City (UNESCO) Bauhaus / International Style flat, built 1937",
   "reinforced-concrete frame with silicate brick and coarse sand block walls, smooth white render",
   "thin 6 / 12 cm plastered sand-block partitions",
   "3.10 m ceilings with 35 cm down-stand beams",
   "recessed street loggia with a dropped ceiling hiding the roller-shutter (trisim) boxes",
   "wide, low strip windows (179 x 67 cm, sill 1.40 m) in the east bedrooms",
   "shared rear utility balcony (mirpeset sherut) with the kitchen door and the bath and WC windows",
   "terrazzo floors and original built-in cupboards; originally centrally heated (radiator recesses survive)",
   "no mamad: the building's shelter is two basement rooms reinforced with steel beams and sheet steel"
  ],
  "notes": "A 1937 Bauhaus flat on the top floor of the Max Liebling House, with thin sand-block partitions, brick facades and a concrete stairwell wall along the living room. With the router by the front door, the hall, study, kitchen and bedroom D are fine and the living room is borderline (about -67 dBm, behind the stairwell corner), but bedroom C at the far corner, the bath and WC, the street loggia and the shared utility balcony sit two to four walls away and are the weak spots; a second access point or mesh node in corridor I by the bedrooms is the usual fix. The thin old partitions probably lose less than the 12 dB used here, so a site survey may read better."
 },
